course: dictionary
lesson: 11727
title: A strong dislike
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: The word aversion means a strong dislike of something, or a feeling that you want to keep away from it. You will often see it with to, as in an aversion to. «Mia has an aversion to cats, so she crosses the road when one walks by.» Mia does not just dislike cats a little: she avoids them.
explain: Look at what the person does for clues. If someone runs, hides or refuses, the word is probably about avoiding something. «Tom has an aversion to hard work, so he always finds a reason to stop early.» The clue is finds a reason to stop, which shows he wants to avoid the work.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Replace aversion with dislike, and see if the sentence still makes sense. «Tom has a dislike of hard work» still works, so dislike is a good everyday word for it. Aversion is the more formal word, and it sounds stronger.
fact: Aversion is a noun. Its plural is aversions.
fact: «Aversion» means a strong dislike of something.
fact: «Aversion» can also mean a feeling of wanting to avoid something.
fact: Useful phrases: an aversion to, as in «an aversion to loud noises».
fact: In science, animals can learn an aversion to food that tastes bad, so they stop eating it.
exercise: «The dog had an aversion to baths and hid under the bed whenever the tap ran.» What does aversion mean here? | A need for more water | A small, soft bed | *A strong dislike of baths | A love of swimming
exercise: «Ella has an aversion to spiders, so she runs out of the room whenever one appears.» What does this tell us? | *Ella wants to avoid spiders | Ella collects spiders | Ella is a scientist who studies spiders
exercise: Which word could replace aversion in «He has an aversion to cold showers»? | wish | joy | *dislike | music
test: «Dad's risk aversion means he always checks the weather before a trip.» What does risk aversion mean? | Making money fast | *Avoiding chances and danger | Playing games | Loving adventure
test: «The fox learned an aversion to the bitter berries.» What did the fox learn? | To eat more berries | *To stay away from the bitter berries | To grow taller | To sing loudly
exam: Which word best replaces aversion in «She has an aversion to shouting»? | gift | *dislike | sleep | dance
